[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Android View Measure, Layout, and Draw Pass Internals"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every UI element on an Android screen goes through a strict traversal lifecycle managed by the Android UI framework. This lifecycle consists of three distinct phases: Measure, Layout, and Draw. Understanding how these phases work internally is crucial for building performant custom views and avoiding nested layout bottlenecks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Measure Pass"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The measure pass answers the fundamental question: 'How big should this view be?' It starts from the root of the View hierarchy and traverses down. Every View receives its dimensions through a combination of its own LayoutParams and the constraints imposed by its parent via the MeasureSpec class."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"EXACTLY: The parent has determined an exact size for the child (e.g., match_parent or a fixed dp value)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AT_MOST: The child can be as large as it wants up to a specified maximum size (e.g., wrap_content)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UNSPECIFIED: The parent has not imposed any constraints on the child. This is commonly used in internal framework measurements like ScrollView."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onMeasure(int widthMeasureSpec, int heightMeasureSpec) {
    int desiredWidth = 200;
    int desiredHeight = 100;

    int widthMode = MeasureSpec.getMode(widthMeasureSpec);
    int widthSize = MeasureSpec.getSize(widthMeasureSpec);
    int heightMode = MeasureSpec.getMode(heightMeasureSpec);
    int heightSize = MeasureSpec.getSize(heightMeasureSpec);

    int width;
    int height;

    if (widthMode == MeasureSpec.EXACTLY) {
        width = widthSize;
    } else if (widthMode == MeasureSpec.AT_MOST) {
        width = Math.min(desiredWidth, widthSize);
    } else {
        width = desiredWidth;
    }

    if (heightMode == MeasureSpec.EXACTLY) {
        height = heightSize;
    } else if (heightMode == MeasureSpec.AT_MOST) {
        height = Math.min(desiredHeight, heightSize);
    } else {
        height = desiredHeight;
    }

    setMeasuredDimension(width, height);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Layout Pass"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once every view has been measured, the layout pass begins. The layout pass answers the question: 'Where should this view be positioned?' Like the measure pass, this traverses top-down. ViewGroup subclasses are responsible for invoking layout() on each of their children, passing the relative left, top, right, and bottom coordinates."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onLayout(boolean changed, int left, int top, int right, int bottom) {
    int count = getChildCount();
    int currentTop = getPaddingTop();

    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        View child = getChildAt(i);
        if (child.getVisibility() != GONE) {
            int width = child.getMeasuredWidth();
            int height = child.getMeasuredHeight();

            // Position the child vertically inside the custom ViewGroup
            child.layout(getPaddingLeft(), currentTop, getPaddingLeft() + width, currentTop + height);
            currentTop += height;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Draw Pass"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"With dimensions and locations finalized, the draw pass renders the UI to the screen. The Android framework executes drawing in a specific sequence to ensure proper layering:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Draw the view's background."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. If necessary, save the canvas layer to prepare for fading edges."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Draw the view's content (via onDraw())."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. Draw children (dispatchDraw())."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"5. Draw decorative elements like scrollbars."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onDraw(Canvas canvas) {
    super.onDraw(canvas);

    Paint paint = new Paint();
    paint.setColor(Color.BLUE);
    paint.setAntiAlias(true);

    // Draw a custom circle in the center of the view
    float centerX = getWidth() / 2f;
    float centerY = getHeight() / 2f;
    float radius = Math.min(centerX, centerY) / 2f;

    canvas.drawCircle(centerX, centerY, radius, paint);
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Performance Optimization Guidelines"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Deep view hierarchies can cause multiple measure and layout passes, severely dropping your frame rate (dropping below 60 or 120 FPS). To optimize rendering pipelines, flatten your view hierarchy using ConstraintLayout, avoid heavy object allocation inside onDraw() or onMeasure() to prevent garbage collection stutter, and trigger manual invalidation only when properties genuinely change using invalidate() for redraws or requestLayout() for remeasuring."}]
